      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          No, not all Ryobi tools are brushless. In the 18V ONE+ range, the brushless models are the ONE+ HP tools, such as the PBLDD02 drill that Ryobi rates at up to 850 in-lbs of torque; the budget ONE+ tools without the HP badge use brushed motors. The battery is the same either way. This page shows how to tell them apart and when the upgrade is worth paying for.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Are Ryobi Tools Brushless: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Only some of them. Ryobi sells its 18V ONE+ tools in two tiers. The ONE+ HP tools use brushless motors, and Ryobi puts both "HP" and "Brushless" in their product names. The standard ONE+ tools, the cheapest ones in the orange aisle, mostly use brushed motors, and their names do not say brushless. Both tiers run on the same 18V ONE+ batteries. The 40V outdoor line is a separate battery system with its own brushless and brushed models.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ryobi tool</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Motor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ryobi's rating</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">PBLDD02 ONE+ HP 1/2-inch drill/driver</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brushless</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 850 in-lbs, 0-700 / 0-2,150 RPM</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">PBLDD01 ONE+ HP 1/2-inch drill/driver</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brushless</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 750 in-lbs, 0-500 / 0-2,100 RPM</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">PCL206 ONE+ 1/2-inch drill/driver</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brushed (not sold as brushless)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 515 in-lbs, 0-450 / 0-1,750 RPM</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">PBLID01 ONE+ HP 1/4-inch impact driver</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brushless</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 2,200 in-lbs, 0-2,900 RPM</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">PCL235 ONE+ 1/4-inch impact driver</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brushed (not sold as brushless)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 1,800 in-lbs, 0-2,700 RPM</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">PBLIW01 ONE+ HP 1/2-inch impact wrench</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brushless</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,170 ft-lbs breakaway, 700 ft-lbs fastening</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            All figures in that table are Ryobi's own ratings from its product pages, not measurements. Ryobi measures torque its own way, so use them to compare Ryobi tools with each other, not to rank Ryobi against Milwaukee or DeWalt. For how the HP drill stacks up against other brands, see our <a href="/best-cordless-drill/">best cordless drill guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="all-one-plus" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Are All Ryobi ONE+ Tools Brushless?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No. ONE+ is the name of Ryobi's 18V battery platform, not a motor type. It covers hundreds of tools, from drills and saws to inflators, fans, glue guns and string trimmers, and only part of that catalog is brushless. Ryobi built the platform around affordable brushed tools first and added brushless motors later, mostly under the ONE+ HP name. Both kinds are still on sale side by side, often on the same shelf, which is exactly why the question comes up so often.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brushless models are concentrated where a motor works hardest: drills, impact drivers, impact wrenches, circular saws, reciprocating saws, grinders, rotary hammers and the bigger outdoor tools. Ryobi also makes compact HP versions of the drill and impact driver that are shorter and lighter than the full-size HP models. On the brushed side you will find the entry drill and impact driver, many specialty and light-duty tools, and most of the low-draw gadgets like lights, fans and speakers, where a brushless motor would add cost without adding anything you would notice.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical answer to "are all Ryobi ONE+ tools brushless" is this: assume a ONE+ tool is brushed unless its name or packaging says brushless. That default will be right almost every time, and it protects you from paying HP money for a basic tool that a retailer has photographed next to an HP battery.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-tell" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Can You Tell If a Ryobi Tool Is Brushless?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Read the product name. Ryobi names its brushless tools plainly, for example "18V ONE+ HP Brushless 1/2 in. Drill/Driver" and "18V ONE+ HP Brushless 1/4 in. Impact Driver" on its own site. If the word brushless is missing from the full product name on ryobitools.com or the retailer listing, treat the tool as brushed. The basic PCL206 drill page, for example, describes "a powerful motor" and never calls it brushless.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Look for the HP badge on the tool</h3>

          <p>
            ONE+ HP tools carry an HP mark on the housing and the packaging, and Ryobi describes the HP line as pairing brushless motors with advanced electronics and its high-performance lithium batteries. The badge is the quickest check in a store aisle or a photo of a used tool. Standard ONE+ tools carry the ONE+ logo without the HP mark.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use the model number as a hint, then confirm</h3>

          <p>
            Ryobi's current HP drill and impact model numbers start with PBL (PBLDD02 drill, PBLID01 impact driver, PBLIW01 impact wrench), while the current basic drill and impact driver start with PCL (PCL206 and PCL235). That pattern is handy, but Ryobi does not publish it as a rule, and other product families use other prefixes. Type the exact model number into ryobitools.com and read the name before you rely on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brushless-vs-brushed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Brushless vs Brushed Motor Drill: What Is the Difference?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A brushed motor uses small carbon brushes that rub against a spinning commutator to switch the current through the motor's windings. A brushless motor does that switching electronically, with a circuit board that reads the rotor's position and times the current itself. Nothing rubs, so less energy turns into friction and heat, and the tool's electronics can adjust power to the load. That is true of every brand, not only Ryobi.
          </p>

          <p>
            What that gets you, in general terms: more of the battery's energy goes into turning the bit, so a brushless tool typically runs longer per charge and holds its speed better when the load goes up, such as a spade bit in a wet joist or a long lag screw. Brushless tools are often more compact for the same power, because the motor can be smaller. And there are no brushes to wear out, so one of the classic service items on a brushed tool simply disappears.
          </p>

          <p>
            How much difference you get depends on the specific tools being compared, which is why we do not quote a runtime percentage of our own. Makers do quote figures against their own older tools. Ryobi, for example, says the HP PBLDD01 drill delivers up to 20 percent faster drilling and up to 50 percent more torque compared with its older P215 brushed drill, and that the PBLID01 impact driver's brushless motor delivers up to 25 percent faster driving. Those are Ryobi's claims, measured its own way.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Brushless matters most on tools that work hard for long stretches.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Drills on big bits, impact drivers on long screws, saws, grinders and trimmers gain the most. A brushed motor is perfectly adequate for a light that sips power, a glue gun, or a drill that hangs pictures twice a month.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Brushed motors still have real advantages: they cost less to build, so the tools are cheaper, and the technology is simple and proven. The brushes on a homeowner's drill can last for years of occasional use. If you own a brushed drill that works, it is not obsolete; it just has less in reserve when the job gets heavy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ryobi-drill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is the Ryobi ONE+ Drill Good?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, for household work, with the right model. The ONE+ HP brushless drill is the one we recommend. Our pillar guide names the PBLDD02 as its value pick; it is Ryobi's current full-size HP brushless drill and the successor to the PBLDD01. Ryobi rates the PBLDD02 at up to 850 in-lbs of torque with a two-speed gearbox of 0-700 and 0-2,150 RPM, a 24-position clutch and a 1/2-inch chuck, and it is sold bare or in a kit with a 4Ah battery and charger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryobi now marks the bare PBLDD01 as discontinued on its site, though remaining kits can still turn up at retailers. If you find one at a good price, it remains a capable drill: Ryobi rates it at up to 750 in-lbs with 0-500 and 0-2,100 RPM and the same 24-position clutch. Both carry Ryobi's 3-year warranty. Compare what battery comes in the box, because a small 2Ah pack runs down quickly on hole saws.
          </p>

          <p>
            The basic brushed ONE+ drill, the PCL206, is a different proposition. Ryobi rates it at up to 515 in-lbs with 0-450 and 0-1,750 RPM, and it is one of the cheapest ways into a name-brand 18V platform. Owners commonly report it is fine for furniture assembly, hanging shelves, small pilot holes and occasional deck screws. Where it struggles is sustained heavy drilling, such as large spade bits or hole saws through framing, where a brushless drill keeps its speed and the brushed one slows and warms up.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the answer to "is the Ryobi ONE+ drill good" depends on which one. For a first drill that will see decks, fences and remodeling, spend the extra on the HP brushless model. For a drawer-and-shelf drill, or a second drill to keep a driver bit in, the brushed one is good value. Our <a href="/ryobi-tools-review/">Ryobi tools review</a> covers the rest of the range in the same way: the platform is strong, but not every tool in it is.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="impacts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Are Ryobi ONE+ Impact Drivers and 1/2-Inch Impact Wrenches Brushless?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The HP ones are. The PBLID01 is Ryobi's ONE+ HP brushless 1/4-inch impact driver; Ryobi rates it at up to 2,200 in-lbs of torque, 0-2,900 RPM and up to 4,000 impacts per minute, with a 3-year warranty, sold as a bare tool. The basic PCL235 impact driver is the non-HP option, rated by Ryobi at up to 1,800 in-lbs and 0-2,700 RPM. An impact driver of either kind will sink deck screws and lag bolts that make a drill's clutch slip, but the brushless model has more in reserve on long structural screws.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are searching for Ryobi ONE+ 1/2-inch impact reviews, you are usually after an impact wrench for lug nuts and suspension bolts rather than a driver. Ryobi's top 18V model is the PBLIW01, an HP brushless 4-mode 1/2-inch high-torque impact wrench that Ryobi rates at 1,170 ft-lbs of breakaway torque and 700 ft-lbs of fastening torque. Ryobi also sells a smaller compact HP brushless 1/2-inch wrench for tighter spaces and lighter work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Owners commonly report that the HP impact wrench handles car and light-truck lug nuts with ease, which is the job most homeowners buy it for. Two cautions apply. First, breakaway torque is a loosening figure; never use an impact wrench at full power to tighten lug nuts. Run them on snug, then finish with a <a href="/best-torque-wrench/">torque wrench</a> to your vehicle's spec, or you risk stretched studs and warped rotors. Second, use impact-rated sockets only; chrome hand sockets can crack.
          </p>

          <p>
            For impact drivers across every battery platform, including how Ryobi's HP model compares with Milwaukee, DeWalt and Makita, see our <a href="/best-impact-driver/">best impact driver guide</a>. Wear safety glasses with any impact tool; bits and fasteners can snap under hammering loads.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="batteries" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. ONE+ HP vs ONE+ vs 40V: Which Ryobi Batteries Fit Which Tools?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every Ryobi 18V ONE+ battery fits every 18V ONE+ tool, HP or not. Ryobi states it directly on its product pages: any 18V ONE+ battery works with any 18V ONE+ product. That includes the HP high-performance packs, which fit the old brushed tools, and the older ONE+ packs, which fit the new HP brushless tools. This backward compatibility is the single best thing about the platform and the main reason people stay with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fit is not the same as performance, though. Ryobi says its HP tools are designed to get the most from its high-performance batteries, and our <a href="/ryobi-tools-review/">Ryobi review</a> notes that high-draw HP tools reach their best results on the larger HP packs. A small, old 1.5Ah pack will run an HP circular saw or impact wrench, but not for long, and it will sag under load. If you buy a high-draw HP tool, budget for at least one 4Ah or larger pack.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ryobi line</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Battery</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Motors</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fits other lines?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">18V ONE+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18V ONE+ packs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mostly brushed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shares batteries with ONE+ HP</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">18V ONE+ HP</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Same 18V ONE+ packs (HP packs recommended)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brushless</td>
