                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">40V (and 40V HP)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40V packs only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brushless and brushed, by model</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No; does not take 18V packs</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The 40V line is a separate system for mowers, blowers, chainsaws, snow blowers and other big outdoor tools. A 40V battery will not fit an 18V ONE+ tool, and an 18V pack will not fit a 40V tool. The 40V HP models are brushless, and as with the 18V side, Ryobi puts HP and brushless in the names, so the same reading-the-name rule applies. If you need both, plan for two chargers and two battery families.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="is-ryobi-good" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is Ryobi ONE Plus Good, and When Is a Brushed Tool Fine?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryobi ONE+ is a good platform for homeowners and a weaker one for daily professional use. The range is enormous, the batteries are cheap and backward compatible, and the HP brushless drills, impact drivers and saws are strong enough for decks, fences, furniture builds and most remodeling. Where Ryobi falls behind Milwaukee, DeWalt and Makita is durability under daily jobsite abuse, the top end of power, and the feel of the trigger and clutch on the basic tools. If you are deciding between the pro brands instead, read <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            A brushed ONE+ tool is a perfectly sensible buy in several cases. If the tool runs for a few minutes at a time, such as a light, a fan, an inflator, a glue gun or a hand vacuum, a brushless motor adds cost you will never notice. If it is a second drill that will hold a driver bit while the HP drill holds a drill bit, brushed is fine. And if the budget is tight, a brushed drill now beats an HP drill you put off buying for a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spend on HP brushless where the motor works hard and long: the main drill, the impact driver, the circular and reciprocating saws, the grinder, and the string trimmer. Those are the tools where owners commonly report the clearest difference, and where a brushed motor is most likely to bog down, heat up and wear its brushes. The price gap between the brushed and HP versions of these tools is usually modest compared with the life you get from them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes With Ryobi Brushless Tools
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Every ONE+ Tool Is Brushless</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                ONE+ is the battery platform, not the motor. Most of the cheapest ONE+ tools are brushed, and the brushless ones say HP and brushless in the name. If the listing does not say brushless, assume it is brushed and price it accordingly before you compare it with an HP kit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Battery in the Photo</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An HP battery fitted to a basic tool in a product photo or a used listing does not make the tool brushless. Check the badge and the model number on the tool itself, then look it up on ryobitools.com. Sellers often pair the best battery they own with whatever tool they are selling.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Ryobi Torque Numbers With Other Brands</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ryobi's in-lbs ratings come from its own test method, and Milwaukee, DeWalt and Makita each use theirs. A higher number on a Ryobi box does not prove it out-twists a pro drill. Use the ratings to compare Ryobi tools with each other and read owner reports for the rest.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running HP Tools on Tiny Old Packs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Old 1.5Ah ONE+ batteries fit HP brushless tools, but they cannot feed a saw or an impact wrench for long, and power sags under load. Owners who call an HP tool weak are often running it on the smallest pack they own. Add at least one 4Ah or larger pack for high-draw tools.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Tightening Lug Nuts With the Impact Wrench</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A brushless 1/2-inch impact wrench is rated for far more torque than a wheel needs. Running lug nuts down at full power can stretch studs and warp rotors. Snug them with the impact, or by hand, then finish with a torque wrench set to the figure in your owner's manual.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are all Ryobi ONE+ tools brushless?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. ONE+ is Ryobi's 18V battery platform, and it includes both brushed and brushless tools. The brushless models are mostly sold as ONE+ HP and say brushless in the product name. The budget ONE+ drill, impact driver and many light-duty tools use brushed motors. All of them take the same 18V ONE+ batteries.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Ryobi ONE+ HP always brushless?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ryobi describes its ONE+ HP line as combining brushless motors with advanced electronics and high-performance batteries, and its HP tools are named as brushless on its site. If you see the HP badge on a current Ryobi tool, it is a brushless model. Still confirm the exact model number on ryobitools.com if you are buying used or from a third-party seller.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do ONE+ HP tools work with old Ryobi batteries?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Ryobi states that any 18V ONE+ battery works with any 18V ONE+ product, so older packs fit the HP brushless tools. They will not deliver the tool's best performance on high-draw jobs, though. Ryobi says HP tools are built to get the most from its high-performance packs, so pair saws and impact wrenches with a larger pack.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a brushless drill worth it for a homeowner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a main drill that will see decks, fences, hole saws and remodeling, yes; brushless typically gives more runtime per charge, holds speed under load and has no brushes to wear out. For light jobs like hanging pictures and assembling furniture, a brushed drill does the work for less money. Many homeowners own one of each.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you replace the brushes on a Ryobi brushed drill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On many brushed tools the carbon brushes are a wear part that a service center can replace, but on budget cordless tools the repair often costs close to a new bare tool. For a homeowner's drill, the brushes usually outlast the battery and the chuck under occasional use. If a brushed drill sparks heavily or loses power, check it under the 3-year warranty first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Ryobi 40V batteries fit ONE+ tools?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Ryobi's 40V outdoor line uses its own batteries, which do not fit 18V ONE+ tools, and 18V packs do not fit 40V tools. The 40V range includes brushless HP models and some brushed ones, and like the 18V tools, the brushless 40V models say so in the product name. Plan for two chargers if you own both.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Ryobi ONE+ good compared with Milwaukee or DeWalt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For homeowners, Ryobi ONE+ HP brushless tools are good value and handle most household projects. Milwaukee and DeWalt cost more and are built for daily jobsite use, with more power at the top end and tougher housings. If you work with tools for a living, a pro brand is the better long-term platform; for weekend projects, Ryobi is enough.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

